# Major and minor told apart on the page: a chord's bottom-to-middle gap of four semitones or three,
# on the keyboard, the staff and the guitar, and a tune's third note that follows it.
lesson music-major-and-minor v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=1 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Major and minor"
  goal "Tell a major chord from a minor one by counting the semitones from its bottom note to its middle note."

  look {
    scene 35x23 {
      row all space=2 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        column keys space=1 align=start {
          text maj "C major"
          piano k1 from=C4 whites=8 wide=2 labels=letters down=[C4, E4, G4]
          text min "C minor"
          piano k2 from=C4 whites=8 wide=2 labels=letters down=[C4, Ef4, G4]
        }
        narrowboat boat windows=5 pots=3
      }
    }
    say """
      On a narrowboat moored by the lock, Ben plays two chords on a small keyboard. Each chord is
      three keys: a bottom note, a middle note and a top note. The bottom and top notes are the same
      in both. Only the middle note moves. In C major it is four semitones above C, on E. In C minor
      it is three semitones above C, on the black key below E, called E flat. Many people hear major
      as bright and minor as darker, but the keys tell you which is which without hearing a note.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.minor-play-middle count=1 seed=4 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked music.minor-which-chord k=3 level=medium
    }
    practice music.minor-which-chord count=1 seed=5 easy-seed=4 hard-seed=6
    practice music.minor-play-triad count=1 seed=2 hard-seed=2
    show music.minor-which-chord k=7 easy-k=2 hard-k=8
    practice music.minor-frere-jacques count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.minor-guitar-string count=1 seed=1
    practice music.minor-strum count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=1
    level hard {
      show music.minor-claim q=1 r=0
    }
    practice music.canal-chart-minor count=1 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.minor-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.minor-white-triads v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Bottom to middle: four semitones is major, three is minor."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that major and minor are a measurement before they are a feeling: the gap from a
    chord's bottom note to its middle note is four semitones in a major chord and three in a minor
    one, and the top note stays where it is. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting white keys
    only, counting the key you start on, and deciding by whether a chord has a black key in it. In
    the working, look for a finger moving key by key, black keys included, from the bottom note. The
    two-star question tests the black-key idea against A minor and D major, and the core already
    asks about B, D, F sharp, which is minor with a black key; the three-star question builds a
    chord on every white key and sorts them, and the chord on B is neither. A child who is stuck can
    play C major and C minor one after the other on any keyboard, point at the key that moved, and
    go back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if the count still starts on the bottom
    key. Off the page, sing a tune you both know in a minor key by lowering its third note, as
    Mahler did with Frère Jacques, and ask which version suits which moment.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the chords to count and to name are all white keys, one is worked in the teacher's pen, the chord to play is A minor, C major or D minor, Frère Jacques is in A, the guitar pair is E minor and E major and the finger is lifted to make the minor chord, the chart question counts the verse's minor bars, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star counts the major chords on the white keys."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the middle key to find is a black key, the chords to name include ones with a sharp, and one of them is minor, B minor or F sharp minor, so a sharp alone never says major, the chord to play needs a black key, Frère Jacques is in E where F sharp is in both versions, the guitar pair is A minor and A major and a finger is added to make the major chord, a claim about moving the top note is judged before the two-star, the chart question counts over the whole song, and the three-star asks how many of the seven white-key chords are neither major nor minor."
  }
}
